structuredContent.text:
### Using the theme() function

Since v4 includes CSS variables for all of your theme values, we recommend using those variables instead of the `theme()` function whenever possible:

```css
/* [!code filename:CSS] */
.my-class {
  /* [!code --:2] */
  background-color: theme(colors.red.500);
  /* [!code ++:2] */
  background-color: var(--color-red-500);
}
```

For cases where you still need to use the `theme()` function (like in media queries where CSS variables aren't supported), you should use the CSS variable name instead of the old dot notation:

```css
/* [!code filename:CSS] */
@media (width >= theme(screens.xl)) { /* [!code --] */
@media (width >= theme(--breakpoint-xl)) { /* [!code ++] */
  /* ... */
}
```

content.text:
Use `@variant` with the breakpoint name inside your custom CSS, and set the variable in that block:

```css
.my-element {
  --my-var: 1rem;
  @variant md { --my-var: 2rem; }
}
```

- The `@variant` directive applies any Tailwind variant in custom CSS. The docs show it with `dark` and `hover`, not with a breakpoint. I'm inferring that `md` works the same way, since breakpoints are variants too. (`src/docs/adding-custom-styles.mdx`, "Using custom CSS → Using variants", lines 299–400)
- If you need a raw media query, use `@media (width >= theme(--breakpoint-xl)) { ... }`. This is the v4 form of `theme(screens.xl)`. CSS variables can't be used inside media queries. (`src/docs/upgrade-guide.mdx`, "Changes from v3 → Using the theme() function", lines 807–829)

